  "answer": "I'll start by reading the skills that apply to this feature, then look through the storefront cart API and how store settings are loaded.The request already spells out the limit, the config key, the default, and the error behavior, so I\u2019ll implement it with tests first. I\u2019m looking up the storefront cart endpoints and how merchant settings are read.The limit belongs in a small check that reads the store configuration on each request. I\u2019ll write that test first, then wire it into the cart create and update paths before anything is saved.The storefront cart now rejects any line that would hold more than `CART_MAX_LINE_QUANTITY` units. That value is read from the store's merchant configuration on each request (default 99 when the key is missing), and a quantity of 0 still removes the line. Create, add, and quantity-change requests that would go over the limit return HTTP 400 and leave the cart unchanged.\n\nVERIFICATION: {\"ran\": [\"mvn -pl sm-shop -am -Dtest=CartLineQuantityLimitTest -Dsurefire.failIfNoSpecifiedTests=false test -o\"], \"result\": \"passed\", \"could_not_verify\": [\"HTTP 400 on the live cart endpoints and that an over-limit request leaves a persisted cart unchanged\"], \"missing\": [\"a storefront cart integration test that sets CART_MAX_LINE_QUANTITY and posts over-limit quantities\"]}",
